    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah.jpg" alt="Yusuf Adil Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_House_of_Bijapur_Album_leaf,_ca._1680_Deccan,_Bijapur._Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamal Muhammad; Chand Muhammad, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> was one of five states that emerged from the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, which had ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> since 1347. As the Bahmani sultans lost control to their great nobles in the late fifteenth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Khan</a>, governor of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, made himself effectively independent around 1489–1490, while at first still acknowledging the Bahmani ruler. His origins are uncertain: the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Ferishta</a> repeats a story that he was a son of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, but he was more probably a Turkman or Persian who came to the Deccan as a young man and rose in Bahmani service. He made the old fortified town of Bijapur, on the plateau between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhima-i-chaulukya-dynasty">Bhima</a> rivers, his capital, and extended his rule across the western Deccan to the coast, where he held the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>. Yusuf was a Shia and, according to Ferishta, introduced the Shia form of the Friday sermon in his realm, an unusual step in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the time. He died in 1510, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> seized Goa for Portugal. His successors took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a>, and the dynasty, the Adil Shahis, held Bijapur for nearly two centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bijapur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bijapur.jpg" alt="Bijapur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bijapur_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montage of several images, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> ruled a mixed society. The court and army drew on three main groups: Persian, Turkish and Arab immigrants known as Westerners (<em>gharibs</em>), Deccani Muslims long settled in the region, and Africans, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddis">Habshis</a>, many of whom had arrived as slaves and rose to high command. The great majority of subjects were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> peasants speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, and the countryside was administered through hereditary local officials, the Marathi-speaking deshmukhs and deshpandes, whose cooperation the sultans needed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-i">Ibrahim Adil Shah I</a> (r. 1534–1558) is credited with making Marathi a language of revenue administration alongside Persian. The economy rested on the black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> soils of the plateau, which produced cotton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and wheat, and on textile weaving and the trade of ports on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dabhol">Dabhol</a>. Bijapur itself grew into a large walled city with markets, mosques, palaces and an elaborate water supply of tanks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and channels, among them the great stepwell known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-bawdi">Taj Bawdi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shaikhs, studied by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-eaton">Richard Eaton</a>, had important roles at court and among the people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</a>, who praised the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> in his songs, was remembered for his openness towards Hindu subjects, musicians and scholars.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque.jpg" alt="Afonso de Albuquerque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Afonso_de_Albuquerque_(ap%C3%B3s_1545)_-_Autor_desconhecido.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s armies were built around cavalry raised by nobles holding land assignments, supported by infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and substantial artillery. The sultanate was almost constantly at war. Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> to the south over the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> doab between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a> rivers. In 1510 Bijapur's forces briefly retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>, but the Portuguese captured it again in November that year, and later efforts to recover it, notably the great siege of 1570–1571 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a>, failed. In January 1565 Bijapur joined Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar in the coalition that crushed Vijayanagara at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a>, opening the south to expansion. Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji Bhonsle</a>, conquered much of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> in 1638 and the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> in 1649. The massive walls of Bijapur, with great bastions mounting heavy guns such as the bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-i-maidan">Malik-i-Maidan</a>, protected the capital, which resisted a Mughal army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-i">Jai Singh</a> in 1665–1666 before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1686.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i.jpg" alt="Ali Adil Shah I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aliadilshah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Adil Shahs</a> ruled as absolute monarchs with Persianate court institutions inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmanis</a>. Under the sultan stood a chief minister, ministers for finance and the army, and provincial governors and commanders paid through land assignments. Persian was the language of high government and diplomacy, while the Dakhni form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> became a language of court literature. The dynasty's religious policy shifted with its rulers: Yusuf and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-adil-shah">Ismail Adil Shah</a> favoured Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Persian immigrants; Ibrahim I turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and the Deccanis; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a> (r. 1558–1580) restored Shia observance; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</a> (r. 1580–1627), who succeeded as a boy under the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-bibi">Chand Bibi</a>, his uncle's widow, favoured Sunni Islam while patronising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> musicians and scholars. Factional conflict between Westerners, Deccanis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddis">Habshis</a> repeatedly paralysed government during royal minorities. Hindu chiefs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> families, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji Bhonsle</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, served as commanders and revenue officials, a dependence that later helped Shivaji build his own power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s western territories. From 1636 the sultans also had to manage the overbearing suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, whose envoys at Bijapur watched over the payment of tribute and the conduct of foreign policy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza.jpg" alt="Ibrahim Rauza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza">Ibrahim Rauza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_Roza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aanand, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s greatest legacy is its architecture and art. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza">Ibrahim Rauza</a>, the tomb and mosque complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</a> completed in the 1620s, is admired for its refined carving and proportions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a>, the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a> (r. 1627–1656), carries a dome about 44 metres across on the outside, one of the largest masonry domes of the pre-modern world, with a famous whispering gallery. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-bijapur">Jami Masjid</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asar-mahal">Asar Mahal</a> and the city walls complete one of the richest groups of Islamic monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ibrahim II, a musician and poet, wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-i-nauras">Kitab-i-Nauras</a></em>, a collection of songs in Dakhni set to Indian ragas that opens with an invocation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a>, and his court produced a distinctive school of Deccani painting. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Muhammad Qasim Ferishta</a> wrote his celebrated history of Muslim India under his patronage. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-ii">Ali Adil Shah II</a> the poet Nusrati wrote some of the major early works of Dakhni literature, a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> poetry. Politically, Bijapur's part in the defeat of Vijayanagara reshaped the south, and its employment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> nobles and soldiers gave the future Maratha state a training ground in war and administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="Shah Jahan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Jujhar_Singh_Bundela_Kneels_in_Submission_to_Shah_Jahan%27,_painted_by_Bichitr,_c._1630,_Chester_Beatty_Library_(cropped2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bichitr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s decline came from the pressure of two rising powers. The Mughal conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> left Bijapur exposed, and in 1636 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a> accepted a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> that recognised Mughal overlordship and required payment of tribute, in return for part of Ahmadnagar's lands. Bijapur compensated by expanding south, but after Muhammad's death in 1656 the succession of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-ii">Ali Adil Shah II</a> prompted a Mughal invasion under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a> in 1657. At the same time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, son of the Bijapur noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji</a>, carved a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> kingdom out of Bijapur's western lands, killing the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afzal-khan-general">Afzal Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a> in 1659 and defeating further expeditions sent against him. Factional strife among the nobility worsened under the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-adil-shah">Sikandar Adil Shah</a>, who came to the throne in 1672, and Bijapur's southern conquests slipped away to local commanders and to the Marathas. In 1685 Aurangzeb, now emperor, came south in person and his armies besieged Bijapur. After a siege of more than a year, with the defenders weakened by hunger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> surrendered in September 1686. He died a prisoner in 1700, and Bijapur became the seat of a Mughal province; the following year Aurangzeb annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> of the Adil Shahi dynasty, one of the successor states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, dominated the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> for nearly two centuries, helped defeat Vijayanagara at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Talikota</a> in 1565 and left monuments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a> before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1686.</em></p>

